    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-6810",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14077511/assets/4969bf3cea8290c76e79419ed0160a8c1f92d7415250305df56eb8128ad15cf4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+t-1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{4{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)+1} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
